WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.660
Film Courage: In the filmmaking process for
JOKER was there one scene (maybe the beginning

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:08.560
of principal photography) where you said “This
is going to be great!”

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.320
And it energized you and kept you on a momentum?

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.880
Lawrence Sher, ASC: Yes.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.519
Honestly it was like the first shot that we
did.

00:00:16.519 --> 00:00:21.939
The first shot we did shooting the movie was
him [Joaquin Phoenix as Joker] at the social

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:25.289
worker’s scene which is like the second
scene in the movie.

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:31.259
So when he is laughing, that big laughing
spell and then she starts asking him about

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:40.649
the job and that whole scene which is about
seven or eight pages or so…actually both

00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:44.149
social worker scenes and then when he comes
back the second time and he says “You never

00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:45.149
listen.”

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:51.410
And all of that, we shot both those scenes,
it was like 10 pages of work in one day, not

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.879
even a long day.

00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:59.210
But that first take of him performing I went
“This is going to be pretty great.”

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:05.159
I was like “This movie could be divisive
because it’s darker than people expected

00:01:05.159 --> 00:01:08.159
or maybe not as funny as they would expect
from Todd [Phillips].”

00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:14.750
I knew there would be some things in which
you go “We might not get all of the Batman

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.960
and the DC [Comics] fans on board.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.149
But what we’re going to make is going to
be something that we’re really proud of

00:01:20.149 --> 00:01:27.640
simply because I could see it from that performance
and honestly I would go home and excitedly

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:33.040
show the dailies to my wife and just because
I was so excited to show her what we were

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.040
doing.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:42.189
So I was feeling pretty strong and pretty
high about the movie from the beginning.

00:01:42.189 --> 00:01:49.610
Then it was really motivating because I recognized
“Oh, wait…so we can’t let off of the

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:50.610
gas…ever.”

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:54.530
As opposed to if you start slowly and you
start to get up to speed and by two weeks

00:01:54.530 --> 00:02:00.530
in you’re like “Oh now I understand the
movie and you start ramping and every day

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:01.979
you’re trying to make it better and better.”

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:04.329
Here I was like “Oh shoot, Day one was great.

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:08.509
Day two was great, Day three also great scene.”

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:10.700
Now it’s like every day has to be this good.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:17.130
So then it was a motivating to keep hyper-focused
on making something that I thought could be

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.840
certainly the best thing Todd and I had done
together.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.310
And I wanted it to be the best thing I had
ever done.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.310
I just did.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:30.480
It was definitely a thing where I wanted this
to be the best work I’ve ever done.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:37.090
And so I just stayed super-focused everyday
to not let off of the gas.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:42.140
I was feeling pretty positive about the movie
right away.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.180
Film Courage: How much of the film is improvisational?

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.500
Lawrence: I don’t know how much is improvisational?

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:54.769
There is a lot of improvisation in the way
that we don’t rehearse and we don’t put

00:02:54.769 --> 00:02:56.330
down marks.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:02.650
We just start shooting the scene and witnessing
it in real time and that way it is very improvisational.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:06.430
As far as within the body of the scene, not
a ton.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:10.780
We did some scenes which were just ideas for
things, which were just ideas for things which

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:15.830
were not necessarily in the script when we
first started that would just become sort

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:23.010
of ideas that we could do like in the apartment
and things like that.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:30.370
And Joaquin was certainly fine to go off of
the script a little bit.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:36.550
Like for instance when he stood up and was
playing with the and was talking to himself

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:39.519
like “Oh you’re a really good dancer.”

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:44.160
That wasn’t in the script it was just happening
in real time and may have been discussions

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.440
from Todd but for me and my team it was like
this is happening so let’s just photograph

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.990
it but generally speaking Todd is not one
of those improv guys who goes “Now try this,

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:55.990
now try that.”

00:03:55.990 --> 00:04:00.659
If we’re going to change the script he changes
it with the actors and the writer and he kind

00:04:00.659 --> 00:04:05.769
of works it out a new version of the scene
and then will just carry on from there and

00:04:05.769 --> 00:04:09.159
do that version as opposed to sort of changing
it within.

00:04:09.159 --> 00:04:15.250
The real improvisation is sort of in technique
and fluidity in which me and my camera operator

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:21.981
we tell the story as we would shoot the scenes
and the fact that we would wouldn’t rehearse

00:04:21.981 --> 00:04:27.490
or put down marks or that sort of thing so
we would just make the movie a bit improvisationaly

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.440
at times which was maybe half the time.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:35.740
Film Courage: Was it in the script that you
weren’t sure if scenes were in Arthur’s

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.400
mind or really happening?

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:41.190
Lawerence: I think that is just part of the
script inherently right?

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:46.910
And part of the fact that the Joker (even
in the lore of the comic books) is an unreliable

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:52.760
narrator of his own life so he will tell stories
and it’s like “Was that a truthful story?”

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:57.070
I’m not a big comic book guy but I know
from reading stuff and talking to people that

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:03.270
he’ll tell backstories about himself in
comic books and graphic novels in which every

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:05.310
time he tells a story it’s something different.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:09.550
I think to create this idea that is he ever
being truthful?

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:10.550
Is any of it true?

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:11.550
Is none of it true?

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:12.550
That kind of thing.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:19.480
So I think inherent to the script certainly
you’re left to question and ask yourself

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:22.390
“Is that real?”

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:26.330
Obviously we do it within the body of the
movie with the Sophie character [Zazie Beetz]

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:34.810
and that relationship to allow us to sort
of see a manifestation of that idea in a relationship

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:39.970
that he fantasizes about and that yet we’re
seeing it in a way that it looks like it’s

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:40.970
happening.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:47.550
So I think it is meant to allow you to have
an interpretation as the audience to decide

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.250
what is real and what is not.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:53.880
But it’s certainly not something that we
wanted to key in and tell the audience precisely

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:59.230
what was and what is wasn’t because I think
we want the audience to have their own interpretation.

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:07.120
Film Courage: Right and then the talk show
rehearsing for it and kind of being his best

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:13.280
self…and no spoilers…but going there and
not knowing what was real.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:21.090
Lawrence: Yes and it was one of those things
that again TAXI DRIVER has been used as a

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:27.080
template for the movie I think obviously because
it’s a disaffected guy who is doing a job

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:31.060
and sort of strikes out against society a
little bit and has some of those correlations.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:38.170
But even that final scene in TAXI DRIVER when
he picks up Cybil Shepherd in the taxi cab

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:40.350
is up for interpretation, right?

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:43.720
I think when I watched it for the first time,
I questioned that interpretation.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:54.150
But when I watched it two weeks ago you recognized
how even how potentially obvious the stylistic

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:59.050
aspects of some of the camera work in that
scene could lead you to believe that “Oh

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:06.670
did Travis Bickle perish in that scene in
the lair of the Jodie Foster character.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:13.930
And this whole post script of him being a
hero and getting the letter from Jodie Foster’s

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:21.860
parents and is that all just a dream or just
a fantasy of his afterlife or whatever?

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:29.020
When I watched it when I was in film school
back in college I didn’t necessarily go

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:30.020
to that interpretation.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.070
But I recognize that people do.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:35.630
So I think similarly I think people can do
the same thing here if they choose to.

